
Abstraction
Hiding the implementation details and revealing only the functionality to the user.

Abstract Class (0–100% abstraction):
- Can have abstract methods.
- Can have concrete methods.
- Can have constructors, variables, and static methods.
- Cannot be instantiated.


Interface (100% abstraction in the traditional sense):
- A class implements an interface and provides the method implementations.

Interface
│
├── Cannot Create Object
├── Supports Abstraction
├── Supports Multiple Inheritance
├── Methods are Public and Abstract by Default
└── Variables are Public Static Final by Default

1. Interface Variables
Interface variables are implicitly public, static, and final.
2. Interface Methods
Interface methods are public and abstract by default.


